course: dictionary
lesson: 4751
title: Hostage: held until someone gets what they want
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A hostage is a person who is held prisoner by someone who will not let them go until they get what they want. When you meet this word, look at the words around it for clues: who is being held, and what is being asked for.
explain: «Hostages' families campaigned to bring them home.» Families want people back home, so a hostage is a person kept away from home. The word families is a clue that the hostages matter to people who love them.
explain: The word can also be used in a figurative way. «Held hostage by the weather» means the weather controls what you can do, like a prisoner who cannot leave. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "controlled by the weather" still make sense? If it does, you have understood it.
fact: A hostage is a person held prisoner until someone gets what they want.
fact: To take someone hostage, or hold someone hostage, means to capture and keep them like this.
fact: Taking someone hostage is a crime under the law of every country.
fact: In stories, a hostage raises the stakes, so the hero's task becomes more dangerous and important.
fact: «Held hostage» can also mean controlled by something, as in «held hostage by the weather».
exercise: What is a hostage? | A bird that sings at night | A tool used for digging holes | A person who sells food at a market | *A person held prisoner until someone gets what they want
exercise: «Held hostage by the weather» means the weather is | *Controlling what you can do | Hidden inside a box | Moving very quickly | Pleasant and warm
exercise: Which phrase is about setting free people who are being held? | climb a tree | *release the hostages | take a photo | blow out the candles
test: In a story, why does a hostage matter? | It is a kind of food eaten at dinner | It shows the story is about the weather | It means the hero is bored and sleepy | *It makes the hero's task more dangerous and important
test: Which sentence uses hostage correctly? | The hostage was a bright red bicycle. | We went to the park to hostage the ducks. | *The robbers held the bank manager hostage until the police arrived. | The robbers ate a hostage for their dinner.
exam: What does "hold someone hostage" mean? | *To capture and keep someone prisoner until you get what you want | To hide a secret in a drawer | To give someone a present on their birthday | To race someone to the finish line
